WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
MongoDB security should show up in everyday pull requests.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:16.920
Review direct driver calls, Mongoose queries, raw commands, filters, updates, aggregations, repository helpers, migrations, seed scripts, exports, and admin tooling.

3
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:30.620
A reviewer should be able to answer what collection is accessed, what query shape is allowed, what input controls it, what authorization applies, what fields return to the caller, which database user runs the operation, and how errors are handled.

4
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.460
Tests should cover both allowed behavior and rejected behavior.

5
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:49.640
Exercise expected filters, invalid types, rejected operators, unauthorized document access, tenant boundaries, unsafe update fields, aggregation behavior, projection behavior, and safe error responses.

6
00:00:49.740 --> 00:01:00.460
CI/CD can support the workflow with unit tests, integration tests, linting, dependency checks, secret scanning, and deployment configuration review.

7
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:07.200
These tools help catch recurring mistakes, but they do not replace human review of business authorization and data exposure.

8
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.960
Monitoring closes the loop after deployment.

9
00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:23.020
Logs should capture useful security events such as denied access, rejected filters, validation failures, suspicious query patterns, admin operations, backup failures, and connection errors.

10
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:32.240
Avoid full sensitive documents, credentials, tokens, cookies, connection strings, private URLs, and unnecessary personal data.

11
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:43.780
Watch for unusual query volume, repeated authorization failures, unexpected exports, slow queries, connection spikes, backup issues, privilege changes, and administrative activity.

12
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:54.920
Incident response should include query-path review, affected collection analysis, credential rotation, access-log review, backup review, and remediation validation.
